cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Value-Field Theory (VFT)"
message: "If you use this theory or repository, please cite this repository."
authors:
  - family-names: "Nuno"
    given-names: "T."
    affiliation: "Independent Researcher"
abstract: >
  Value-Field Theory (VFT) is a structural framework for describing how a
  shared multidimensional real-resource world K, actor-specific accessible
  portions K_i, and actor-specific expectation-forming states P_i condition
  actions and decisions A_i. K is not treated as a separate resource world for
  each actor; actor relativity enters through access to K, through P_i, and
  through actor-specific expectations E_i[Delta K]. Delta K denotes the realized
  real-resource quantity difference over a defined target scope and interval.
  The same Delta K type is used across micro and macro scales; what changes is
  the actor set, time horizon, resource scope, and aggregation rule. E_i[Delta K]
  is a rational expected interval change for the resource scope in which actor i
  participates through production, consumption, or related action. Preferences,
  desirability, credit, beliefs, and related structures remain in P_i rather than
  being identified with E_i[Delta K]. Longer horizons and larger populations may
  make aggregate expectations reflect persistent demand and supply structures,
  but demand and supply are projection-level interpretations rather than core
  definitions of E_i[Delta K]. Aggregate expected resource change can be written
  as a compatible aggregation of E_i[Delta K]. Mapping this aggregation to
  standard economic surplus requires additional valuation, utility, WTP/WTA, or
  cost assumptions. Observable actions, Delta K, Delta P, and nominal-economic
  indicators may be used as proxies subject to domain-specific measurement
  assumptions.
repository-code: "https://github.com/nuno929/vf-model"
